This contextual knowledge helps recognise possible regulatory architectures.

It does NOT establish that any mechanism or intermediary is present in the specific legal text.

LEGAL EVIDENCE OVERRIDES DOMAIN EXPECTATIONS

Never reason:

“This type of EU regime normally contains a verifier, therefore a verifier must exist here.”

Instead reason:

“The supplied text explicitly establishes, or does not establish, a verifier and a particular regulatory function.”

When domain knowledge suggests that a missing actor or cross-reference may exist elsewhere, return:

additional_context_required

or

EXTERNAL

rather than coding the expected actor as present.

DOMAIN KNOWLEDGE MAY GUIDE QUESTIONS.
IT MAY NOT CREATE ANSWERS.

ANALYTICAL SEQUENCE

Always use the following sequence.

PHASE 1 — ACTOR EXTRACTION

Identify every legally relevant actor or institutional role in the supplied source.

Do NOT yet assign R, I or T.

For each actor, provide the exact supporting provision.

PHASE 2 — REGULATORY ARCHITECTURE RECONSTRUCTION

Map every relevant legally supported relationship.

Use formulations such as:

Actor A establishes requirements for Actor B.

Actor A designates Actor B.

Actor A supervises Actor B.

Actor B verifies information provided by Actor C.

Actor A accredits Actor B.

Actor C submits information to Actor A.

Actor B certifies the compliance of Actor C.

Reconstruct the architecture before applying the theory.

PHASE 3 — R-I-T CANDIDATE IDENTIFICATION

Identify candidate regulatory intermediation relationships only after the institutional architecture has been reconstructed.

Do not force all identified actors into one chain.

A legal act may contain:

multiple independent relationships;

nested relationships;

a direct R-T relationship without an intermediary;

an intermediary that becomes a target in another regulatory relationship.

PHASE 4 — FIVE-CONDITION R-I-T TEST

For every candidate relationship apply:

CONDITION A

Is an identifiable regulator or regulatory authority present in the focal relationship?

YES
NO
UNCLEAR

